/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  landscape06_nitrogen_fixation_routes
  ====================================

  THREE OVERALL ROUTES TO FIXED NITROGEN, PRICED ON ONE SURFACE.  The sixth
  witness of the EduTool `equilibrium-landscapes`
  (gui/src/ui/methods/EquilibriumLandscapesTool.tsx) and the first case of
  the `reactionGibbs` property operation (src/propertyOps/ReactionGibbs.cpp),
  which exists because of it.

  WHY A NEW OP WAS NEEDED, measured before it was written.  The engine's one
  route to a reaction's Gibbs energy, `Reaction::equilibrium`
  (src/thermo/reaction/Reaction.cpp), prices every species on the ideal-gas
  rung with `g_pure_ig(T)`, and that rung REFUSES a record tabulated on the
  solid standard state.  A gibbsMap with glucose beside the gases was run
  and refused, verbatim: "Component 'glucose': h_pure_ig(T) evaluates the
  IDEAL-GAS rung, but this record declares standardThermochemistry.
  referenceState pureSolid -- dHf_298 and s_298 are tabulated on the solid
  standard state, so reading them here would be wrong by a heat of
  sublimation."  The same refusal binds `equilibriumReactor` and
  `gibbsReactor`.  The biological routes are exactly a solid sugar and
  liquid water beside gases, so no reactor and no existing op could price
  them; `reactionGibbs` sums a DECLARED reaction over the phase-aware
  `h_formation` / `s_formation` surface the Component already carried.

  THE THREE ROUTES (constant/reactions), balanced by ELECTRON COUNT -- the
  biological ones carry NO H2 (Vitor's correction, 2026-09-27: nitrogenase
  reduces N2 with protons and electrons that come from the organism's
  substrate):
      heterotrophic   C6H12O6(s) + 4 N2 + 6 H2O(l) -> 6 CO2 + 8 NH3
      phototrophic    N2 + 3 H2O(l) -> 2 NH3 + 3/2 O2
      haberBosch      N2 + 3 H2 -> 2 NH3
  Every species is priced on a DECLARED standard state (`phases {}`): the
  sugar on its own pureSolid rung, water as a pure liquid reached by the
  vaporisation crossing from its ideal-gas datum (announced), the gases on
  idealGas.  The lesson's expectation, stated before the run: the sugar
  route is EXERGONIC (respiration pays for fixation), the water route is
  ENDERGONIC (light pays), the industrial route is exergonic at 298 K and
  loses that with temperature -- the landscape witness beside this case
  draws the loss.

  TWO TEMPERATURES, AND WHY ONLY THE FIRST IS ASKED OF THE SUGAR ROUTE.
  298.15 K is the datum temperature, where a declared rung answers with no
  heat capacity integrated.  glucose.dat carries NO heat-capacity block on
  any rung ("FLAGGED, NOT FABRICATED", its own words), so asking the
  heterotrophic route at 310.15 K REFUSES by name -- measured, verbatim:
  "Component 'glucose': h_formation liquid leg needs liquidHeatCapacity"
  (the solid leg falls back to the liquid Cp and finds none).  The refusal
  is the finding and is quoted on the page; this case therefore prices all
  three routes at 298.15 K in one op and the two routes that can move in
  temperature at 310.15 K in a second op, so that the run passes and the
  gap is stated rather than papered over; a third op prices the industrial
  route alone at 700 K, the converter temperature of gibbs10, where its dG
  has changed sign -- which is the whole reason the converter needs its
  pressure, and the landscape witness beside this case draws it.  Liquid
  water is not asked at 700 K: a pure-liquid standard state above the
  critical temperature is a number with no state behind it.

  NOTHING HERE IS A RATE.  An exergonic route is one the equilibrium
  permits; what makes each one go -- the enzyme's ATP, the light, the iron
  catalyst's temperature -- is outside every record in this tree, and the
  ATP stoichiometry of nitrogenase is deliberately NOT cited because no
  reference for it exists in the repository.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name         routes298;
        type         reactionGibbs;
        reactions    ( heterotrophic phototrophic haberBosch );
        phases
        {
            glucose  pureSolid;
            water    pureLiquid;
            N2       idealGas;
            NH3      idealGas;
            CO2      idealGas;
            O2       idealGas;
            H2       idealGas;
        }
        temperatures ( 298.15 );                    // K: the datum temperature
        output       { file routes298.csv; }
    }
    {
        name         routes310;
        type         reactionGibbs;
        reactions    ( phototrophic haberBosch );   // the sugar route refuses here (no Cp on glucose)
        phases
        {
            water    pureLiquid;
            N2       idealGas;
            NH3      idealGas;
            O2       idealGas;
            H2       idealGas;
        }
        temperatures ( 310.15 );                    // K: body temperature
        output       { file routes310.csv; }
    }
    {
        name         haber700;
        type         reactionGibbs;
        reactions    ( haberBosch );                // the industrial route at its converter temperature
        phases
        {
            N2       idealGas;
            NH3      idealGas;
            H2       idealGas;
        }
        temperatures ( 700 );                       // K: gibbs10's converter; the landscape witness draws the loss
        output       { file haber700.csv; }
    }
);
